## 1. Introduction
Self-assembly is an intrinsic property of collagen, which is a major structural protein in most of mammalian tissues [1,2]. In vivo, self-assembly of collagen begins with three amino acid peptide strands that adopt a polyproline type II helical structure. Then, they wind around each other to form the well-known triple helix. These triple helices can pack in a quasi-hexagonal and staggered fashion to form nanofibrous structures known as collagen fibrils, which continue to self-assemble both linearly and laterally to form collagen fibers and a hydrogel network [3,4]. In vitro, free collagen with triple-helical structure can also spontaneously assemble to form fibrils with structural, mechanical, and biological properties similar to those observed in vivo [5]. It is well known that thermal stability of the triple-helical structure and self-assembly behavior of collagen are strongly affected by temperature [6]. Above the melting temperature $(T_{\mathrm{m}})$ , triple-helical structure of collagen converts to the random coil conformation, resulting in disappearance of the self-assembly ability. Self-assembly is promoted by the thermodynamically driven intermolecular interaction involving hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions [7]. Similarly, the thermal dissociation of collagen fibrils may also be a thermodynamically driven endothermic process. There may be a thermal dissociation critical temperature (TDCT), above which (but below $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ of collagen) the intermolecular interaction of collagen fibrils may be disappear, and the fibrils will convert to free collagen molecules again. Although in recent years, much attention has been given to fibrillogenesis of collagen [8] and to the collapse of triple-helical structure of the collagen molecule [9], the thermally induced dissociation of collagen fibrils has not been elucidated.
The fibrillogenesis kinetics of collagen are commonly monitored by turbidity measurement based on light transmittance as a function of time [10]. Nevertheless, the details, such as the number, size, and connections of collagen fibrils cannot be sensitively analyzed by the turbidity assay [11]. It is well known that mechanical properties are an important factor to consider when collagen is applied as bioengineering materials. Hence, self-assembly dynamics and evolution of mechanical properties of collagen during gelation have been tracked by oscillatory rheology [12]. The gelation dynamics of pure collagen and composition of collagen and other extracellular-matrix components have been studied by some researchers [13–15]. Because oriented D-staggered striates can spontaneously form via self-assembly of collagen, the fibrillogenesis process may be analyzed by scanning electron microscopy, transmission electron microscopy, or timelapse confocal reflectance microscopy [11]. Thioflavin T (Th-T) is a particularly useful fluorescent dyes that helps to distinguish fibrils and monomers of amyloid [16–18] and has been widely used to monitor amyloidogenesis in vitro [19,20]. Morimoto et al. reported that Th-T can specifically bind to collagen fibrils thus showing enhanced fluorescence emission at $485\mathrm{nm}$ with excitation at $430\mathrm{nm}$ . No enhancement of fluorescence is observed in the mixture with free collagen molecules or denatured collagen [21]. The specific fluorescence enhancement by Th-T binding to collagen fibrils is suitable for monitoring fibrillogenesis and dissociation of collagen fibrils.
In this study, Th-T was used as a fluorescent probe to monitor the dynamic process of collagen self-assembly and dissociation. The accuracy and sensitivity of the fluorescence method based on Th-T were evaluated and compared with those of turbidity assay. $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ of pepsin-soluble collagen (PSC) was measured by circular dichroism (CD) spectroscopy to obtain the range of operating temperatures of the thermal dissociation experiments. After that, the dynamic process of collagen dissociation was studied by the fluorescence method, and the data were verified by morphological analysis of fibrils and the proportion of retained fibrils. Finally, the thermal stability of triple-helical structures of free-, assembled-, and dissociated- PSC was compared. Thus, we demonstrated the fibrillogenesis of collagen and thermal dissociation of collagen fibrils in vitro. These data may advance the understanding of fibril formation and inverse dissociation of fish-sourced collagen in vitro.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Preparation of PSC from snakehead skin
Live snakeheads (Channa argus) were purchased at a supermarket in Wuhan (Hubei, China). The skin was removed manually, and washed with chilled tap water. The PSC was extracted from the skin of snakeheads according to the method of Yang et al. [22]. The collagen was identified as type I collagen by sodium dodecyl sulfate polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE; data not shown).
## 2.2. Fibrillogenesis kinetics of PSC
2.2.1. Fluorescence spectroscopy of Th-T, PSC, and a mixture of PSC and Th-T (named PSC/Th-T) for fibrillogenesis analysis
The PSC solution $1.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ in $0.5\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid) was prepared and dialyzed against phosphate buffer (PBS, $200\mathrm{mM}$ , pH 7.4) at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 2 days. After that, the solution of PSC $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL})$ and PSC/ThT (final concentrations $0.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ and $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) in PBS was prepared and incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 60 min in total darkness to induce fibrillogenesis and obtain assembled-PSC and assembledPSC/Th-T. In addition, the assembled-PSC/Th-T was dissociated at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 60 min to obtain dissociated-PSC/Th-T. Their fluorescence was measured (excitation at $421{\mathrm{nm}}$ ) on a fluorescence spectrophotometer (Varian, Cary Eclipse, USA) by the method of Morimoto et al. with a minor modification [21]. Similarly, fluorescence of PSC $\mathrm{\langle0.5mg/mL\rangle}$ and PSC/Th-T (final concentrations $0.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ and $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) was monitored under the same conditions. Additionally, the fluorescence of a Th-T (Acros) solution (final concentration $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ in PBS), incubated for various periods at $50^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ was also measured to examine its thermal stability. All fluorescence measurements were carried out at the rate of $600\mathrm{nm/min}$ , and slit openings for both excitation and emission were fixed at $5\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.2.2. The turbidity assay of PSC and PSC/Th-T for fibrillogenesis analysis
The turbidity versus time curves during fibrillogenesis of PSC and PSC/Th-T were drawn by the method of Aukkanit et al. [23]. The fibrillogenesis of PSC $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL})$ and PSC/Th-T (final concentrations $0.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ and $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) was initiated by incubation at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The kinetics of fibrillogenesis was monitored by recording absorbance at $310\mathrm{nm}$ as a function of time on a UVspectrophotometer (UV-2000, UNICO, Shanghai, China) [10].
## 2.2.3. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) of PSC during fibrillogenesis
AFM was used to examine the morphological features of collagen fibrils at the initial stage (0 s) and nucleation stage (180 s) of the fibrillogenesis process. The PSC solution $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL})$ was incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 0 and 180 s and diluted 5–7 fold immediately with deionized water in an ice-bath. Then, the diluted samples were immediately deposited onto newly cleaved mica and airdried at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight. Imaging was carried out by AFM (AFM, SPM9700, Shimadzu, Japan) in tapping-mode, typically using a square pyramidal silicon nitride tip with the nominal spring constant of ${\sim}0.58\ensuremath{\mathrm{N/m}}$ . All samples were imaged at room temperature, and force mode images were taken simultaneously.
## 2.2.4. The degree of fibrillogenesis of PSC during the fibrillogenesis process
The degree of fibrillogenesis of PSC samples at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at various time points was examined by the method of Bae et al. [24]. The PSC solution $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL})$ was incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 1300, 1500, 1600, or 1800s, respectively. Then, the resulting gel was centrifuged immediately at $10000\times g$ for $10\mathrm{{min}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The concentration of 4-hydroxyproline in the supernatant was determined according to the method of Reddy et al. [25]. The degree of fibrillogenesis $(\mathrm{DF\%})$ was calculated as follows:
