## 2.3.2. Fluorescence spectroscopy of assembled-PSC/Th-T for analysis of thermal dissociation
To explore heat-induced dissociation behavior of collagen fibrils by the fluorescence method, the solution of PSC/Th-T (final concentrations $0.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ and $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively) was incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ in total darkness to obtain assembled-PSC/ThT with fibril structures. After that, the fluorescence intensity of assembled-PSC/Th-T versus time was measured. In addition, TDCTs of assembled-PSC (assembled for various periods) were determined by this method.
2.3.3. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) of assembled-PSC during dissociation
The morphological features of fibrils of dissociated-PSC under different conditions were examined by SEM and TEM. First, the PSC solution $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ in PBS) was incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain assembled-PSC. For SEM analysis, the dissociation of assembled-PSC was carried out by incubation at $36^{\circ}C$ and $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 10, 30, 60, and $90\mathrm{{min}}$ . Subsequently, these samples were centrifuged at $10000\times g$ for $10\mathrm{{min}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The supernatant was collected for quantitative analysis of collagen fibrils (see subsection 2.3.4), and the precipitates were used for SEM analysis. These samples were fixed with $2\%(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ glutaraldehyde in PBS for $4\mathrm{h}$ and dehydrated in ethanol of increasing concentrations: $30\%$ , $50\%$ , $70\%$ , $90\%$ , and $100\%\ \mathrm{(v/v)}$ for 5 min each. The resulting samples were dried with critical-point carbon dioxide using a Balzer CPD020 (Blazers, Furstentun, Liechtenstein), mounted on a stub, and coated with a gold/palladium alloy. Then, the morphological characteristics of these samples were analyzed under a Hitachi
4700 scanning electron microscope at $10\mathrm{kV}.$ For each sample, at least three SEM images were randomly captured, and at least 50 fibrils were selected in each image. Fibril diameters were measured in SEM images, and the mean of 150 fibrils was calculated in each case.
For TEM analysis, the assembled-PSC samples were incubated at $36^{\circ}C$ and $43^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ to induce dissociation. Next, these samples were mixed with a small amount of PBS solution to prepare fibril suspensions, which were placed on a copper grid of 200-mesh size. These fibrils were negatively stained with a $1\%$ phospho-tungstic acid solution at $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ for $1\mathrm{min}$ and the stained grids were airdried. The morphological features of the fibrils were examined by TEM (200CX, JEOL) using the accelerating voltage of $75\mathrm{kV}$
## 2.3.4. The proportion of retained fibrils in assembled-PSC during dissociation
To further explore the thermal dissociation of collagen fibrils, the proportion of retained fibrils was determined by monitoring the concentration of 4-hydroxyproline in the supernatant as mentioned in subsection 2.3.3. The proportion of retained fibrils was calculated as follows:
Proportion of ratained fibrils ( $\mathsf{P R F})\%=\left(1-\frac{H_{2}-H_{1}}{H_{1}}\right)\times100\$ where $H_{1}$ and $H_{2}$ are the initial 4-hydroxyproline amounts in the supernatant of a collagen sample before and after thermal dissociation.
