## 2.4. Statistical analysis
All data were subjected to analysis of variance (ANOVA), and the differences between means were evaluated by Duncan’s multiplerange test. The SPSS Statistics software (SPSS 11.0 for Windows, SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, USA) was used for these calculations.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fibrillogenesis kinetics of PSC
3.1.1. Enhanced fluorescence emission of fibril-incorporated Th-T Fig. 1A shows the fluorescence emission spectra of ThT, pure PSC before and after assembly (named PSC and assembled-PSC, respectively), the mixture of PSC and Th-T before and after assembly (named PSC/Th-T and assembledPSC/Th-T, respectively), and the mixture of dissociated PSC and Th-T (named dissociated-PSC/Th-T). Fibril-incorporated Th-T (assembled-PSC/Th-T) showed enhanced fluorescence emission at $485\mathrm{nm}$ with excitation at $421\mathrm{nm}$ according to the method of Morimoto et al. with a minor modification [21] (Fig. 1A). The intrinsic fluorescence intensity of Th-T was similar to that of PSC/Th-T and dissociated-PSC/Th-T. This means that the fluorescence increase was specific to fibril structures rather than to the triple-helical structure [21]. In addition, no fluorescence of PSC and assembledPSC was observed with excitation at $421\mathrm{nm}$ . It is worth noting that the increase in Th-T fluorescence intensity after binding to PSC fibrils was ${\sim}1.6$ , which is similar to the factor of ${\sim}1.5$ reported by Morimoto [21]. This value is far less than the 1000-fold fluorescence enhancement after Th-T binding to amyloid fibrils, probably because of structural differences between collagen and amyloid fibrils. In the Krebs model of Th-T binding to amyloid fibrils, the dye molecule is inserted into the channels formed by side chains of amino acid residues forming $\upbeta$ -sheets [26]. Once the dye is incorporated into amyloid fibrils, the restriction of the rotational motion of Th-T molecules relative to one another in the excited state would result in the obvious enhancement of the fluorescence quantum yield [27]. For collagen fibrils, which differ substantially from amyloid fibrils, no channels can be formed by $\upbeta$ -sheets; however, the hole or gap zone named D-periodic $(67\mathrm{nm})$ can form in the region between the C-terminal end of one molecule and the adjacent Nterminal end of another molecule [28]. These hole or gap regions facilitate the binding of Th-T to collagen fibrils [21] although the affinity is weaker than that of binding to amyloid fibrils. Because the subsequent experiments involved heat treatment, the thermal stability of Th-T was tested. No change in fluorescence intensity of Th-T was observed after heat treatment at $50^{\circ}C$ for various periods (Fig. 1B), suggesting that Th-T is a suitable fluorescent dye for analysis of thermal dissociation of collagen fibrils.
3.1.2. The influence of Th-T on fibrillogenesis of PSC and the corresponding fluorescence kinetics
First, the self-assembly kinetics of PSC and PSC/Th-T were determined at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ by the turbidity assay to elucidate the influence of Th-T on PSC fibrillogenesis. As shown in Fig. 2A, each set of turbidity versus time curves was sigmoid with a nucleation phase, growth phase, and plateau phase. Both sets had the same nucleation stage $(\mathrm{t_{n}},250s)$ and plateau stage $\mathrm{:t_{p}}$ , 1290s). The equilibrium turbidity value of PSC/Th-T was higher than that of PSC; the difference may  result from the intrinsic absorbance of Th-T. These results indicated that fibrillogenesis dynamics of collagen are not influenced by addition of Th-T.
Then, the self-assembly kinetics of PSC were monitored using Th-T as a fluorescent probe. As shown in Fig. 2B, the curve of fluorescence intensity evolution of $\mathbf{psc/In/-T}$ with emission at $485\mathrm{nm}$ and excitation at $421\mathrm{nm}$ , is consistent with the two-step model of nucleation-and-growth observed in the turbidity assay. Nevertheless, the curves of fluorescence intensity evolution of PSC/Th-T showed a shorter nucleation phase $(\mathrm{t}_{\mathrm{n}},180s)$ and longer plateau phase $(\mathrm{t_{p}};1570s)$ , as compared with those of the turbidity-time curve.
To identify the actual nucleation stage of PSC assembly, a real-time morphological analysis of PSC at 0 and 180 s of selfassembly was conducted by AFM. As shown in Fig. 2C and D, there was an obvious difference between morphological features of PSC obtained at 0 and 180 s. At the beginning of self-assembly (0 s), only collagen molecule monomers and small molecule aggregates were detected (Fig. 2C). However, small collagen fibrils with the diameter of $40{-}80\mathrm{nm}$ and length of $300{-}600\mathrm{nm}$ were observed at $180s$ and D-periodicity structure was detectable in some microfibers (marked with an arrow in Fig. 2D). These results indicated that nucleation of collagen had already finished at $180s$ this result confirmed reliability of the nucleation phase data obtained by the fluorescence method. Similarly, to identify the actual plateau stage of PSC assembly, the degree of fibrillogenesis (DF) was utilized to quantitatively analyze fibril formation near the plateau stage. The DF of collagen was $51.5\%\pm1.3\%$ , $54.8\%\pm0.9\%$ , $57.5\%\pm1.5\%$ , and $57.1\%\pm2.1\%$ , when PSC was incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 1300, 1500, 1600, and 1800s, respectively (Fig. 2E, black line). In addition, the DF of PSC/Th-T was also monitored (Fig. 2E, red line), and revealed that the DF of collagen was almost not affected by addition of Th-T. These results indicated that the self-assembly of collagen was continuing after 1300 s (the plateau stage in the turbidity assay) but stopped at ${\sim}1600s$ (the plateau stage according to the fluorescence measurement). These data revealed that the fluorescence method is much more accurate and sensitive for monitoring of the fibrillogenesis dynamics of collagen, as compared with the turbidity assay.
## 3.2. Thermal dissociation of collagen fibrils (assembled-PSC)
## 3.2.1. Melting temperature $\left(T_{m}\right)$ of PSC
Because triple-helical structure and fibrillogenesis of collagen are strongly affected by temperature, thermal stability of PSC was analyzed by CD, and the resulting spectrum was shown in Fig. 3A. The maximal positive peak was obtained at ${\sim}220\mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature (r.t.), which revealed the presence of a triple-helical structure [29]. Notably, the heat denaturation experiment could be finished within ${\sim}120s$ , which was shorter than the nucleation phase of PSC (180 s according to Fig. 2B). Therefore, no collagen fibrils were formed in the heat denaturation experiment, and the CD value reflected the thermal stability of triple-helical structures in our case. The turbidity assay with $0.1\mathrm{mg/mL}$ PSC was also carried out and confirmed the absence of obvious fibrillary aggregation (the inset of Fig. 3B). In addition, CD analyses of PSC fibrils (PSC was incubated for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ ) and dissociated PSC (PSC fibrils were incubated for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ ; this temperature was determined in the fluorescence experiment described below) were conducted, and the results revealed that the CD values were almost not influenced by fibrillogenesis. These CD experiments were repeated in the presence of Th-T, and revealed that the CD values were almost not influenced by the presence of Th-T. The characteristic peaks disappeared in the CD spectrum obtained at $67^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ; this means that PSC was in a completely random conformation. Fig. 3 B shows the corresponding thermal denaturation curve. The curve of the $\mathrm{{CD}_{\mathrm{{T}}}/\mathrm{{CD}_{\mathrm{{r}.t.}}}}$ value vs. temperature has an inflection point at $47^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ which was defined as the melting temperature $(T_{\mathrm{m}})$ of PSC. When the temperature was lower than $47^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , the $\mathrm{CD_{T}/C D_{r.t.}}$ value of PSC almost stayed constant, which means that the triple-helical structures were intact. In contrast, when the temperature was higher than $47^{\circ}\mathsf C$ the $\mathrm{CD_{T}/C D_{r.t.}}$ value of PSC began to drop quickly, because of unwinding of the triple-helical structures. Therefore, the subsequent thermal dissociation experiments with PSC were carried out below $47^{\circ}\mathsf C$ unless stated otherwise.
## 3.2.2. Fluorescence kinetics of collagen fibrils (assembled-PSC/Th-T) during dissociation
Next, thermal dissociation behavior of PSC fibrils was studied at different temperatures. Fig. 4A shows the curves of fluorescence intensity evolution of assembled-PSC/Th-T incubated at 35, 40, and
$45^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The initial fluorescence intensity of assembled-PSC/Th-T was defined as $100\%$ . Each set of fluorescence intensity evolution curves shows that the fluorescence intensity gradually decreases with the extension of incubation duration, and the decrease rate is accelerated by an increase in temperature. Because these experiments were performed below $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ of PSC, the triple-helical structures of PSC should be stable. Therefore, the decrease in fluorescence intensity of assembled-PSC/Th-T can be attributed to the thermal dissociation of fibrils.
The thermal dissociation behavior of PSC, assembled for various periods, was next explored by the fluorescence method to study the impact of assembly duration on the thermal dissociation behavior. In Fig. 4B, the fluorescence intensity-temperature curves of assembled-PSC/Th-T assembled for 1, 12, and $24\mathrm{h}$ are shown. The TDCT of assembled-PSC/Th-T was defined as the inflection point of the fluorescence intensity-temperature curves. Above this temperature, the fluorescence intensity obviously decreased. All of these curves showed a similar TDCT $(39^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , which means that the thermal dissociation was carried out when the temperature was higher than TDCT of collagen fibrils, and TDCT was not affected by assembly duration. The fibrillogenesis of collagen fibrils shows orderly behavior driven by the loss of solvent molecules from the surface of the protein and by the thermodynamically driven intermolecular interaction involving hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and hydrophobic interactions. Similarly, the thermal dissociation of collagen fibrils is also a thermodynamically driven endothermic process, and its TDCT is affected only by intermolecular interactions and not affected by assembly duration.
To confirm that the change in fluorescence was not caused by the simple dissociation of Th-T from collagen fibrils, we evaluated the fluorescence kinetics of dissociation of pig collagen fibrils (inset in Fig. 4B). The fluorescence intensity-temperature curves of pig collagen/Th-T assembled for 1 h showed an inflection point at $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which was defined as the TDCT of pig collagen/Th-T. These results confirmed that the change in fluorescence was not caused by the simple dissociation of Th-T from collagen fibrils.
## 3.2.3. Morphological features of fibrils morphologies in assembled-PSC and dissociated-PSC
The fibril morphology of assembled-PSC during thermal dissociation was studied by SEM. Assembled-PSC was incubated at $36^{\circ}C$ , which was lower than the TDCT $(39^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , and $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which was higher than the TDCT $(39^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , for 10, 30, 60, and $90\mathrm{{min}}$ . The corresponding morphological features of the fibrils were examined by SEM (Fig. 5). The assembled-PSC incubated at $36^{\circ}C$ always yielded identical typical network structures with a gradually increasing diameter of fibrils with the extension of duration of the experiment (from $141.3\pm21.4$ to $156.0{\pm}31.1\mathrm{nm}$ , $p<0.05$ ). This finding suggested that the self-assembly of collagen was still continuing in this process (Fig. 5A-D). In contrast, the fibril diameter of assembledPSC incubated at $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ gradually decreased from $147.9\pm20.9$ to $114.6\pm16.9\mathrm{nm}$ ( $_{.p<0.05)}$ although similar network structures were observed at $36^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Fig. 5a-d). These results indicated that the dissociation of fibrils took place when the temperature was higher than TDCT of assembled-PSC $(39^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . As a control, the fibril morphology of assembled-PSC/Th-T under the same conditions was also assessed and shown as insets; the behavior of these morphological features was similar to that of assembled-PSC.
As shown in Fig. 6, the difference in morphological features of collagen fibrils before and after thermal dissociation was clearly revealed by TEM. At $36^{\circ}C$ , the collagen fibrils continued to assemble, and crude fibrils $(\sim150\mathrm{nm})$ with an obvious periodic banding pattern (D-periodicity) were observed (Fig. 6A). In contrast, at $43^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ the dissociation of collagen fibrils took place, and the crude fibrils and corresponding D-periodicity disappeared; only small fibrils and fragments were detected after $60\mathrm{{min}}$ (Fig. 6B). These results confirmed that the dissociation of collagen fibrils was taking place, when the temperature was higher than TDCT of assembled-PSC.
## 3.2.4. The proportion of retained fibrils in assembled-PSC and dissociated-PSC
Additionally, the proportion of retained fibrils of assembledPSC incubated at $36^{\circ}C$ and $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was quantitatively analyzed, and the results are shown in Fig. 7. The proportion of retained fibrils in assembled-PSC incubated at $36^{\circ}C$ for 10, 30, 60, and $90\mathrm{min}$ were $100.3\%\pm2.5\%$ , $106.1\%\pm6.4\%$ , $108.6\%\pm5.6\%$ , and $107.2\%\pm3.8\%$ respectively. The positive correlation between the proportion of retained fibrils and incubation time (the proportion of retained fibrils was increasing with time) is in agreement with the results of SEM. In contrast, the proportion of retained fibrils in assembledPSC incubated at $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 10, 30, 60, and $90\mathrm{min}$ was $99.7\%\pm1.7\%$ ， $94.4\%\pm2.5\%$ , $52.8\%\pm1.6\%$ , and $38.3\%\pm2.7\%$ , meaning that the number of fibrils decreased with incubation time. These data confirmed that the dissociation of fibrils was taking place when the temperature was higher than TDCT of assembled-PSC, and selfassembly was proceeding when the temperature was lower than TDCT of assembled-PSC. These findings also support the feasibility of the fluorescence method based on Th-T.
## 3.2.5. Thermal stability of assembled-PSC and dissociated-PSC
Thermal stability of the triple-helical structure of PSC, assembled-PSC, and dissociated-PSC with or without Th-T was estimated by DSC, and the results are shown in Fig. 8. All of these thermal transition curves have a typical thermal denaturation peak, which indicates unwinding of the triple-helical structure. The dissociated collagen fibrils (incubation at $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ ) showed the same $T_{\mathrm{m}}\left(47.2^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\right)$ as free collagen molecules; this finding is consistent with the results of the CD analyses $(47^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . On the other hand, assembled-PSC showed a slightly higher $\Gamma_{\mathrm{m}}(48.4^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , which may be attributed to the interaction between collagen fibrils because the dissociation of fibrils could not finish within such a short period in our DSC experiments. These data are consistent with the reports that the thermal stability of triple-helical structures of collagen molecules can be enhanced by molecular interactions. As a control, these DSC experiments were carried out under the same conditions in the presence of Th-T. The maximal endothermic peaks of PSC/ThT, dissociated-PSC/Th-T,  and assembled-PSC/Th-T were similar to those in the absence of Th-T.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, fibirllogenesis of PSC and the inverse thermal dissociation process in vitro were studied by a fluorescence method with Th-T as a probe. Our results indicate that fibrillogenesis dynamics of collagen can be monitored sensitively and accurately by a fluorescence analysis, especially at the nucleation and plateau stages. Moreover, the thermal dissociation behavior of collagen fibrils was explored by this fluorescence method together with SEM, TEM, CD, DSC, and the proportion of retained fibrils. The CD analysis estimated the melting temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ of PSC to be $47^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , and the thermal dissociation experiments were carried out below $47^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to avoid denaturation of collagen. The experiments with fluorescence kinetics at different temperatures revealed that fluorescence intensity gradually decreases with the extension of assay duration, and the decrease rate is accelerated by an increase in temperature. The fluorescence intensity vs. temperature revealed TDCT to be $39^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ; this value was not affected by assembly duration. SEM suggested that the fibrillogenesis of collagen continues when temperature is lower than $39^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and the fibrils dissociate when the temperature is higher than $39^{\circ}C$ . This result was next verified by analysis of the proportion of retained fibrils. The morphological characteristics of fibrils before and after thermal dissociation were clearly revealed by TEM. Additionally, the thermal stability of triple-helical structures of free-, assembled- and dissociated-PSC was compared. The results indicate that the thermal stability of triple-helical structures of collagen molecules can be enhanced by molecular interactions among fibrils. We  confirmed the feasibility of the fluorescence method based on Th-T for analysis of fibrillogenesis and thermal dissociation of collagen. The fibrillogenesis and thermal dissociation processes were characterized in detail, and these data may help researchers to elucidate fibril formation and dissociation of fish-sourced collagen.
